SUMMARY:[Anderson Collection at Stanford University / Office of the Vice P
 resident for the Arts] 1\,000 Ways to Hold: A New Work By Erika Chong Shuc
 h
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260816T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260816T170000
DTSTAMP:20260816T133124Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-52331703768568-52331703861849@local-events
DESCRIPTION:1\,000 Ways to Hold is the culmination of a year-long particip
 atory project by Stanford Arts’ 2025–26 Visiting Artist Erika Chong Sh
 uch\, rooted in conversation and clay. Developed in response to a moment s
 haped by loneliness\, fragmentation\, and uncertainty\, the project propos
 es a tender\, human-scale intervention: two people at a time sit together\
 , shape ceramic bowls in pairs\, and reflect on the question\, What have y
 ou held\, and what has held you? Created across campus in classrooms\, com
 munity sp\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://anderson.stanford.edu/exhibit
 ions/erika-chong-shuch-1000-ways-to-hold/\nLast synced: 2026-08-16 06:31 P
 DT
LOCATION:Anderson Collection
URL:https://anderson.stanford.edu/exhibitions/erika-chong-shuch-1000-ways-
 to-hold/
END:VEVENT
